  run_ids: ["577cf18c-678b-4e75-be43-97ad2194b0e3"] -> ["d6328e5d-b221-407a-9ff1-b193abe1d020"]
  explanation: "byteorder 1.5.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. BE/LE read_u24 additionally returns the exact unsigned24 prefix decode below2^24, and asserts on shorter input. LittleEndian from_slice_u16/u32/u64/u128 additionally leave every slice unchanged on the verified little-endian target, including empty slices and arbitrary unsigned values. The two handwritten Default methods always panic. Four unsigned variable-width readers decode the exact selected prefix under their complete byte-count/input-length guard, and assert otherwise.  Separate Kani checks compare the original BE/LE write_uint/write_uint128 methods with byteorder-lite for all fitting unsigned values and valid byte counts, arbitrary initial 32-byte arrays, and slice lengths from the byte count through 32. Both complete buffers agree and suffix bytes are preserved; four harnesses complete with 456 checks and no failures." -> "byteorder 1.5.0: fixed-width unsigned and signed integer writes, plus BE/LE write_f32/write_f64, write their exact bit representations in the selected endian order and preserve trailing bytes when the output is long enough; shorter buffers panic before writing. Float proofs quantify over every 32/64-bit representation, including NaN payloads and signed zeros, under explicit bit-pattern and std to_bits models. No floating arithmetic is modeled. BE/LE read_u24 additionally returns the exact unsigned24 prefix decode below2^24, and asserts on shorter input. LittleEndian from_slice_u16/u32/u64/u128 additionally leave every slice unchanged on the verified little-endian target, including empty slices and arbitrary unsigned values. The two handwritten Default methods always panic. Four unsigned variable-width readers decode the exact selected prefix under their complete byte-count/input-length guard, and assert otherwise.  Eight additional original concrete reader defaults are verified: BE/LE read_u48 returns the exact six-byte unsigned decode below 2^48; read_int, read_i24 and read_i48 return the selected-width two-complement signed decode. General signed counts must be1..8 with sufficient input; fixed readers need three or six bytes. Failed input/count guards return assertion failure before sign extension. Their original full read_uint callees are connected by definitional equality to the existing byteorder general-reader proofs; the actual original extend_sign body is proved directly with separately named pure bit-vector lemmas, without new models or Lite proof imports. Separate Kani checks compare the original BE/LE write_uint/write_uint128 methods with byteorder-lite for all fitting unsigned values and valid byte counts, arbitrary initial 32-byte arrays, and slice lengths from the byte count through 32. Both complete buffers agree and suffix bytes are preserved; four harnesses complete with 456 checks and no failures."
  evidence_url: "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/f54043136603c4941cdd9ec94c54b11350e932af" -> "https://github.com/nyuichi/rust-crate-proofs/tree/870f2d88bb7608e433038063e4fc4b98b14b1bc3"
  limitations: "This covers 20 selected writer methods, six unsigned readers, four native-target unsigned identity conversions, and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Fixed 16/32/64/128-bit reads, signed readers, variable-width writers, other bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. The signed from_slice defaults contain unsafe slice reinterpretation and are not covered; the floating conversions and opposite-endian/other-target paths are also unproved. The byteorder-lite eight-method result is not assumed transferable across versions. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified. The separate variable-width writer comparison is limited to normal-success inputs and the 32-byte allocation shape. It does not establish failing inputs, arbitrary lengths/allocations, or independent memory safety. It adds no Aeneas API claims." -> "This covers 20 selected writer methods, eight unsigned and six signed readers, four native-target unsigned identity conversions, and two handwritten Default methods, not all public APIs. Fixed 16/32/64/128-bit reads, remaining signed 128-bit readers, variable-width writers, other bulk and in-place conversions, I/O extensions, other targets and release extraction remain unproved. The float result establishes bit serialization only: floating arithmetic, comparisons, rounding, numeric accuracy, NaN arithmetic and signaling behavior are not modeled. Kani public-writer buffer lengths are fixed at 6 and 10; arbitrary valid slice lengths are quantified separately by the Lean theorems. The signed from_slice defaults contain unsafe slice reinterpretation and are not covered; the floating conversions and opposite-endian/other-target paths are also unproved. The byteorder-lite eight-method result is not assumed transferable across versions. Panic messages and unwinding side effects are not specified. The separate variable-width writer comparison is limited to normal-success inputs and the 32-byte allocation shape. It does not establish failing inputs, arbitrary lengths/allocations, or independent memory safety. It adds no Aeneas API claims."
